          "source_text_sha256": "e4ec77998bebc4ee2b38c577cb0235462c1a61c6d00697e21b37d4cbed9bdb31",
          "start_ms": null,
          "status": "machine-unreviewed",
          "text": "Description: An air traffic controller at the Martinique Aimé Césaire International Airport control tower reported an observation made by several people on February 11, 2012, around 11:10 PM. A green glowing phenomenon moved quickly across the Caribbean sky from East to West. Controllers in Guadeloupe, ground gendarmes, and pilots on AFR 3570 (Paris-Martinique) also saw it. The phenomenon of a very bright green sphere crossing the sky in just a few seconds immediately suggests a meteoroid (meteorite) atmospheric reentry or space debris. Space debris enters the atmosphere at approximately 7 km/s and crosses the sky for 20 seconds to a few minutes. Given the observed appearance and duration of the phenomenon, the hypothesis of a meteoroid (meteorite) atmospheric reentry was chosen for this case, classified as \"B.\"",
          "translation_text_sha256": "f74db3e49c361d1e122ecb190f3bec2e1c6747a1b245955303e8462763f05323"
        }
